00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.169
How they use Pub/Sub
in order to do

00:02:04.169 --> 00:02:06.210
push stuff or subscriptions
and things like this.

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:07.890
It's very interesting.

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:09.389
A lot of detail.

00:02:09.389 --> 00:02:10.210
Check it out.

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:11.850
I think it's definitely
worth reading.

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:12.090
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:14.464
They do some interesting stuff
around auto scaling, which

00:02:14.464 --> 00:02:15.810
I thought was interesting.

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:17.330
But I think the
real takeaway here

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:19.994
was they managed to cut their
infrastructure costs in half--

00:02:19.994 --> 00:02:20.910
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:21.750
MARK MANDEL: --doing
this, so I thought

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:22.900
that was pretty impressive.

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:23.816
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:02:23.816 --> 00:02:26.760
There's a line basically saying
that their previous setup

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:30.120
in a Cloud provider competitor
was not elastic enough,

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.700
and that App Engine actually
fixed this because it's

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:33.825
able to scale up and down--

00:02:33.825 --> 00:02:34.170
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:36.128
FRANCESC CAMPOY: --super
fast and super easily.

00:02:36.128 --> 00:02:37.420
So definitely check it out.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:40.160
And if you have
some similar case,

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:43.350
like you have huge peaks
