app, it's definitely

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:49.250
worth to have a look at how App
Engine handles auto scaling.

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:50.250
MARK MANDEL: Absolutely.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:53.110
So the second cool thing of the
week I'm kind of excited by--

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:55.110
it's probably a bit nerdy
but it's kind of cool.

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:56.670
We just announced--
now it is alpha,

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:58.430
so you need to get
whitelisted for it.

00:02:58.430 --> 00:02:59.880
I'll say that up front.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.790
But we just announced Network
Service Tiers, which I think

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:03.690
is super, super cool.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:05.746
So one of the things
with Google Cloud

00:03:05.746 --> 00:03:07.120
is we run our own
network, right?

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.480
It's the same network that
we run for Gmail and Maps

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:10.630
and for everything, right?

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:12.980
This is super fast and super
great except for the fact

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:14.520
it is kind of expensive.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:17.010
Because we run all our
own hardware and we

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:18.570
do a lot of cool stuff for you.

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:21.660
Sometimes, though, you don't
want that super fast network.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:23.910
You want something that's
maybe fairly similar to what

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:25.620
some other Cloud
providers have who

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:26.940
don't run their own networks.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:28.840
And you want it at a
cheaper price point.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:31.800
So now we have this thing called
network service tiers such

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:34.380
that you can have either the
standard, which is our premium

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.660
tier, where you have
great consistency,

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:38.140
great speed, everything else.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:40.290
And then you have
the standard tier,

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:42.397
which uses much more
of the public internet

00:03:42.397 --> 00:03:43.980
and much less of our
network, but that

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:45.900
does mean that you're
paying a whole lot less.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:48.352
So maybe if you're moving
files, like large files,

00:03:48.352 --> 00:03:50.310
from one place to another,
but you don't really

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:51.870
care how quickly they get there.

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:54.251
Like, if they get there in
the next day or so, it's fine,

00:03:54.251 --> 00:03:56.250
so you don't have to pay
those huge egress costs

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:59.550
if you're moving them from,
say, a data center to us or back

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:01.385
again, all sorts of
things like that.

00:04:01.385 --> 00:04:03.885
That means then, yeah, you don't
care as much about latency.

00:04:03.885 --> 00:04:05.910
You don't care necessarily
about consistency

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:07.775
because it is over
the public internet.

00:04:07.775 --> 00:04:09.150
You can save
yourself some money,

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:10.420
so it's actually
really, really cool.

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:12.172
I'm actually really
excited about this.

00:04:12.172 --> 00:04:13.088
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:04:13.088 --> 00:04:15.270
I like that it's actually
configured, I see,

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:17.130
at a resource level.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.350
So you can definitely--
you can basically

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:22.390
decide for every instance
or instance template or load

00:04:22.390 --> 00:04:25.930
balancer, into what network tier
you're going to be using that.

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:28.200
So it's not like
all in, all out.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.685
You could be like, well, you
know, for this specific thing,

00:04:30.685 --> 00:04:33.060
I need to be really fast so
I'm going to go with premium.

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:36.690
With this old thing, it's a map
reduce that might take longer,

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:38.990
I don't care, then you can
go with basic for that.

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:40.700
MARK MANDEL: The right
network for the right job.

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:41.190
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:42.090
Very, very cool.

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:44.100
And also, saving money, which--

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:45.280
I like that.

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:48.630
And then we have the last
cool thing of the week.

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:51.090
Actually, there were way too
many cool things of the week.

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:54.420
But the last one we
chose, it's about Titan,

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:57.950
which both of you and I
have one on our desks.

00:04:57.950 --> 00:04:58.950
MARK MANDEL: Yes, we do.

00:04:58.950 --> 00:04:59.866
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:04:59.866 --> 00:05:02.880
So Titan is a really
cool microchip

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:05.380
that Google designed
specifically

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:07.990
for Google
purpose-built servers.

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:10.300
So it's something that we
run internally at Google

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:12.640
and on Google Cloud, too.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:15.880
And allows us to do really
cool security things

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:17.620
that we thought, this
kind of hardware,

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:19.670
you would not be able to do it.

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:23.630
So there's a new blog post
that covers Titan in depth.

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:25.480
It's definitely in depth.

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:27.010
It's a pretty long one.

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:29.800
But you will be able to
understand not only what

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:31.370
are the basics,
like what is Titan

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:35.080
and how it's built, from a
basic point of view, but also

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:39.130
what it empowers us to do
and what kind of security

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:40.540
we can manage.

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:43.030
For instance, make sure that
if someone accessed something,

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:46.720
we're able to see it, and the
login cannot be tampered with,

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:47.530
stuff like that.

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:51.620
Really, really powerful things
explained in a lot of detail.

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:54.090
And if you are interested
in knowing more,

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:56.470
but you're too lazy
to read, like me,

00:05:56.470 --> 00:05:58.540
you can also listen
to the episode

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:02.200
that we did back in March,
with the final day at Cloud

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:06.050
Next, which was episode 67,
where we interviewed Cornelius

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:08.280
Willis, which is the head
marketing at Google Cloud

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:08.830
Platform.

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:11.980
And Neal Mueller, which
is a product marketing

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:14.170
lead also at Google Cloud.

00:06:14.170 --> 00:06:16.700
And they told us a
little bit about it.

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:19.240
It is a very cool thing
and goes very well

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:20.470
on your earrings, apparently.

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:21.220
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:22.740
No, I love this.

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:24.790
It just goes to
show, I think, what

00:06:24.790 --> 00:06:27.850
levels we go to as a
company to ensure security,

00:06:27.850 --> 00:06:29.640
that we write these
chips ourselves,

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:31.360
build these chips ourselves,
make these chips ourselves,

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:33.610
such that we can be like,
these machines are ours.

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:35.080
The software that's
running on them is ours.

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:36.190
Everything that's
happening on this machine

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:38.687
has not been tampered with
in any way, shape, or form.

00:06:38.687 --> 00:06:40.770
And we make sure that we
own that from end to end.

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:43.014
And that's like,
yay, security, yay.

00:06:43.014 --> 00:06:43.930
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:47.080
It is pretty amazing, but
at the same time, incredibly

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:50.931
important to make sure that your
workloads are running where you

00:06:50.931 --> 00:06:52.180
really think they are running.

00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:52.570
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, exactly.

00:06:52.570 --> 00:06:53.486
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:06:53.486 --> 00:06:54.070
Very cool.

00:06:54.070 --> 00:06:54.610
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:55.110
All right.

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:57.460
Well, why don't we go have
a chat with Michael Will,

00:06:57.460 --> 00:06:59.650
talk to him about his
game "Guardian Kingdoms"?

00:06:59.650 --> 00:07:00.070
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Sounds good.

00:07:00.070 --> 00:07:00.880
Let's do that.

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:02.950
MARK MANDEL: So it is an
absolute pleasure today

00:07:02.950 --> 00:07:06.700
to join one of my friends on
stage from New York, Michael

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:09.240
Will, Director of Technical
Operations at Phoenix One

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:09.740
Games.

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:11.050
Michael, how are
you doing today?

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:12.340
MICHAEL WILL:
Excellent, how are you?

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:13.750
MARK MANDEL: I'm
very, very well.

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:16.090
Thank you so much
for joining us.

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:18.040
Coming to talk to
us about Phoenix One

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:21.072
Games, your newly released
game, "Guardian Kingdoms."

00:07:21.072 --> 00:07:23.280
I don't know if we can still
count it as new anymore.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:25.720
That you have a mobile, but
before we get stuck into that

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:27.400
and how you use
Google Cloud Platform,

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:29.566
why don't you tell us a
little bit about who you are

00:07:29.566 --> 00:07:31.270
and what do you do
at Phoenix One Games?

00:07:31.270 --> 00:07:32.728
MICHAEL WILL: Hi,
I'm Michael Will.

00:07:32.728 --> 00:07:34.450
I work at Phoenix
One Games as Director

00:07:34.450 --> 00:07:35.590
of Technical Operations.

00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:38.020
So I take care of the back
end of the game, basically.

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:40.540
I have a systems engineering
background, hardware--

00:07:40.540 --> 00:07:45.100
I used to make high-performance
compute cluster at Penguin

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:48.100
Computing before, before I
went into the gaming industry.

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:52.000
And so with that focus, I'm
also designing my back end

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.190
and we will talk
about the details

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:55.480
of what that means later.

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:56.396
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:07:56.396 --> 00:08:00.310
So tell us a little bit
about "Guardian Kingdoms."

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:01.770
What it is-- what is it?

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:03.145
MICHAEL WILL:
"Guardian Kingdoms"

00:08:03.145 --> 00:08:05.140
is a real-time
strategy game that's

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:07.900
similar to "Clash of
Clans," in the sense

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:10.930
that you build your own
kingdom and you defend it

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:13.480
against other people and you
build up resources and so on.

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:16.930
But what's unique about
this is that you actually

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:21.939
have massive, real-time gameplay
with three people on your side,

00:08:21.939 --> 00:08:23.230
three people on the other side.

00:08:23.230 --> 00:08:25.400
And all of them drop
hundreds of units,

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:28.600
so your battlefield is full
of thousands of units moving

00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:30.364
and it's pretty exciting.

00:08:30.364 --> 00:08:31.280
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:34.240
So there's basically six
players for every game,

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:35.289
but every player has--

00:08:35.289 --> 00:08:35.890
MICHAEL WILL: Up to.

00:08:35.890 --> 00:08:37.110
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh,
up to six players.

00:08:37.110 --> 00:08:37.580
MICHAEL WILL: So
the thing is you

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:39.620
are organized in
alliances, just like you

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:41.032
would be in those other games.

00:08:41.032 --> 00:08:42.990
But the nice thing is
that if you get attacked,

00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:44.698
they all get notifications
on their phone

00:08:44.698 --> 00:08:46.990
and they can actually
help you and participate.

00:08:46.990 --> 00:08:47.260
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Fun.

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:48.885
MICHAEL WILL: Or they
can just observe.

00:08:48.885 --> 00:08:51.460
So it's like, you might
have 10, 15 people observing

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:55.370
the game and two people on your
side that are supporting you.

00:08:55.370 --> 00:08:57.670
And they can join at any moment.

00:08:57.670 --> 00:09:01.254
So it really can turn also
the game around, which is fun.

00:09:01.254 --> 00:09:02.170
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:05.270
So let's talk a little bit
about how it's implemented.

00:09:05.270 --> 00:09:06.700
It's a mobile game.

00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:09.610
What runs on the mobile,
what runs on the back end?

00:09:09.610 --> 00:09:10.600
Tell us a little bit.

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:11.433
MICHAEL WILL: Right.

00:09:11.433 --> 00:09:13.270
So it's basically a
typical client server

00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:15.610
type multi-tier application.

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:20.080
So the phone, as your client,
it does all the graphics

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:26.020
and it connects through
HTTPS REST to a web farm,

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:28.660
if you want, that implements
the API for the game.

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:32.680
And so anything that your
phone client asks of the game,

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:33.810
the game first validates.

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:35.680
So if you say, I
want to spend 50 gold

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:38.680
on upgrading this tower,
then it will basically

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:41.470
verify that you actually have
that before it does that.

00:09:41.470 --> 00:09:46.330
So the battle simulations are
actually done on battle servers

00:09:46.330 --> 00:09:48.910
that are implemented in C++.

00:09:48.910 --> 00:09:52.510
And we use some artificial
intelligence scripting,

00:09:52.510 --> 00:09:55.810
using Lua technology in
there for best performance.

00:09:55.810 --> 00:09:59.052
And then the back end servers
[INAUDIBLE] from the API,

00:09:59.052 --> 00:10:00.010
basically, to the game.

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:01.300
That's implemented in PHP.

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:02.050
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:10:02.050 --> 00:10:04.250
So I think the
interesting story here

00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:07.280
is that you used
to run, basically,

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:09.362
this whole thing on bare metal.

00:10:09.362 --> 00:10:10.820
And you did that
for quite a while.

00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:13.194
Can you tell us a bit about
what that experience was like

00:10:13.194 --> 00:10:14.600
and what you did there?

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:14.740
MICHAEL WILL: Yeah.

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:17.420
It's more of a hardware systems
engineering approach, right?

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:22.220
So you basically design web
servers, database servers,

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:23.430
and so on.

00:10:23.430 --> 00:10:26.574
And with the servers,
you basically

00:10:26.574 --> 00:10:28.490
look at which ones are
most critical and those

00:10:28.490 --> 00:10:31.380
who make us redundant as
possible inside of the server.

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:35.480
So you basically architect
for server resiliency.

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:37.070
And so for a
database server, that

00:10:37.070 --> 00:10:39.110
means I want to have
redundant power supplies so

00:10:39.110 --> 00:10:41.800
that if one of the power cables
gets disconnected by accident

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:44.570
or one of the power supplies
fails, the system keeps running

00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:46.310
and it can be hot-swapped out.

00:10:46.310 --> 00:10:47.870
And the same goes
for disk drive.

00:10:47.870 --> 00:10:49.580
So there's hardware
rate, which means

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:50.720
you have a bunch of disks.

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:53.630
Data is redundantly
distributed amongst them.

00:10:53.630 --> 00:10:56.030
And so if one of
the disks fails,

00:10:56.030 --> 00:10:57.800
an operator can
replace the disk drive

00:10:57.800 --> 00:10:59.180
without the system
shutting down.

00:10:59.180 --> 00:11:00.860
And it can just keep
writing its data

00:11:00.860 --> 00:11:04.670
and it will reshuffle the data
as a new disk is inserted.

00:11:04.670 --> 00:11:07.460
So hardware systems
engineering focus here

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:11.600
is that you actually
look into the details as,

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:13.190
like, do I want two
rate controllers

00:11:13.190 --> 00:11:14.065
for best performance?

00:11:14.065 --> 00:11:16.880
Do I want to have multiple
volumes and stripe

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:17.660
across those ends?

00:11:17.660 --> 00:11:19.522
So you can do a lot
of optimization there.

00:11:19.522 --> 00:11:21.230
And you also want to
control the firmware

00:11:21.230 --> 00:11:23.540
and be really, very specific
about what you end up

00:11:23.540 --> 00:11:25.700
putting into production,
that you have tested

00:11:25.700 --> 00:11:27.120
and you know it works.

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:30.020
And so that's the hardware
systems engineering

00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:32.210
focus that you have, basically.

00:11:32.210 --> 00:11:35.060
And what's different
now in the Cloud

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:39.469
is you don't have that access
anymore to the detail, which

00:11:39.469 --> 00:11:40.760
at first, seems like a problem.

00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:43.520
But it actually is a
blessing because it allows

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:45.020
you to change your approach.

00:11:45.020 --> 00:11:48.170
So I'm no longer a
hardware system engineer.

00:11:48.170 --> 00:11:50.820
But rather, I'm a
distributed systems engineer.

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:53.990
And so instead of looking at a
single server never going down,

00:11:53.990 --> 00:11:56.750
what I do instead is I look at
the servers never going down

00:11:56.750 --> 00:11:59.750
by having enough servers and
having failover and building

00:11:59.750 --> 00:12:00.650
to my software stack.

00:12:00.650 --> 00:12:01.400
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:02.730
Let's dig deeper into that.

00:12:02.730 --> 00:12:05.750
So you had your hardware.

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:06.710
It was kind of working.

00:12:06.710 --> 00:12:08.360
It seems to me like
it was working.

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:10.100
What were the reasons
why you actually ended up

00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:11.310
transferring over to the cloud?

00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:12.770
I mean, obviously,
you were talking

00:12:12.770 --> 00:12:15.290
about moving to more
of a software focus,

00:12:15.290 --> 00:12:17.264
but were there other
aspects to that, as well?

00:12:17.264 --> 00:12:18.680
MICHAEL WILL: So
a typical problem

00:12:18.680 --> 00:12:21.020
that a startup like
us faces is that we

00:12:21.020 --> 00:12:25.321
need to match our costs with
revenue or potential revenue,

00:12:25.321 --> 00:12:25.820
right?

00:12:25.820 --> 00:12:28.070
So we don't want to
overspend, basically,

00:12:28.070 --> 00:12:29.750
because every dollar
that we don't spend

00:12:29.750 --> 00:12:31.550
on unnecessary stuff
idling around we

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:34.100
can put towards acquiring
more players that actually

00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:35.030
bring revenue.

00:12:35.030 --> 00:12:38.510
So what we found is
that with the hardware,

00:12:38.510 --> 00:12:40.420
it's kind of hard to
scale up and down.

00:12:40.420 --> 00:12:41.960
So we had a nice
contract that would

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:44.862
allow us to say, OK, we don't
need those servers anymore.

00:12:44.862 --> 00:12:46.820
And then they say, OK,
by the end of the month,

00:12:46.820 --> 00:12:48.320
you stop paying for it.

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:51.680
Which is OK, but it basically
makes you think like,

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:52.670
oh it's the 31st.

00:12:52.670 --> 00:12:54.740
We really need to
cancel this server now,

00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:56.690
otherwise we pay
another month for it.

00:12:56.690 --> 00:12:59.510
And so the other thing is if you
want to scale up because you've

00:12:59.510 --> 00:13:02.030
got more players and the
system starts slowing down

00:13:02.030 --> 00:13:04.940
and you're like, oh, I
need 20 more web servers,

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:06.540
they can give them
to you in two days.

00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:08.456
But then you are vetting
them for another week

00:13:08.456 --> 00:13:10.760
and reject some, get
some replacements,

00:13:10.760 --> 00:13:14.480
and get some miscommunication
keyed up and, by the end,

00:13:14.480 --> 00:13:16.100
it's two weeks.

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:17.660
Especially if it's
database servers,

00:13:17.660 --> 00:13:19.130
you will definitely
need two weeks

00:13:19.130 --> 00:13:20.990
to put them into production.

00:13:20.990 --> 00:13:23.240
And so that's
rather slow-moving.

00:13:23.240 --> 00:13:26.540
And so the premise of
the cloud, of course,

00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:29.420
is that you can spin up stuff
in minutes and shut it back down

00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:31.440
and that you only pay
for it when you use it.

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:33.260
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Also, I'm wondering,

00:13:33.260 --> 00:13:34.970
you give a little
bit of the reasoning

00:13:34.970 --> 00:13:39.410
behind moving to the cloud,
but how easy or hard was it?

00:13:39.410 --> 00:13:41.990
Did you need to
rewrite many things

00:13:41.990 --> 00:13:44.270
or basically, all of the
things that you had already

00:13:44.270 --> 00:13:45.165
just worked?

00:13:45.165 --> 00:13:46.790
MICHAEL WILL: It's
basically super easy

00:13:46.790 --> 00:13:50.810
because we were already a
Linux-focused, open source

00:13:50.810 --> 00:13:52.100
architecting shop, right?

00:13:52.100 --> 00:13:55.940
So everything we did was
[INAUDIBLE],, basically.

00:13:55.940 --> 00:14:00.532
Originally, we did really
use Linux, MySQL, Memcache,

00:14:00.532 --> 00:14:01.490
and that kind of stuff.

00:14:01.490 --> 00:14:04.370
And then we replaced MySQL
and Memcache with Couchbase

00:14:04.370 --> 00:14:06.950
at some point to
have less dependency

00:14:06.950 --> 00:14:08.780
on individual
servers going down--

00:14:08.780 --> 00:14:10.490
remember, the original problem.

00:14:10.490 --> 00:14:11.000
And so--

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:12.065
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:14:12.065 --> 00:14:13.940
MICHAEL WILL: --that
all translates very well

00:14:13.940 --> 00:14:16.990
into running it on the Cloud
because it's also Linux

00:14:16.990 --> 00:14:19.560
and I can use the same
services as before.

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:23.150
So one important piece for
PHP developers is knowing,

00:14:23.150 --> 00:14:25.680
how does my code perform,
in general, right?

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:27.440
So if a request comes
in, I want to have

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:30.559
a feel for why a login
is slower or what

00:14:30.559 --> 00:14:32.600
are the slowest requests
that I need to focus on?

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:35.450
And so we use a service
that's called New Relic that's

00:14:35.450 --> 00:14:39.326
basically like PHP Xdebug, but
without the crazy tools that

00:14:39.326 --> 00:14:40.700
take up all your
engineering time

00:14:40.700 --> 00:14:42.470
to evaluate what you found.

00:14:42.470 --> 00:14:47.250
And so what it allows you is
to see statistics in real time,

00:14:47.250 --> 00:14:47.750
basically.

00:14:47.750 --> 00:14:49.320
See, oh yeah, for
some reason, logins

00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:53.480
got slower and purchases of this
one item have really slowed.

00:14:53.480 --> 00:14:56.240
And then you see stack traces
of the actual execution

00:14:56.240 --> 00:14:57.020
of the code.

00:14:57.020 --> 00:15:01.160
You can see, oh, we did
1,000 gets on Couchbase.

00:15:01.160 --> 00:15:03.220
I thought we were
just going to do 10.

00:15:03.220 --> 00:15:04.990
Let me look at the
code and fix this.

00:15:04.990 --> 00:15:09.530
So we can use the same servers
like we used to before.

00:15:09.530 --> 00:15:12.040
So it's dropped in the
same kind of stuff.

00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:12.790
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:15:12.790 --> 00:15:16.415
So which actual GCP products
are you using, then?

00:15:16.415 --> 00:15:18.640
MICHAEL WILL: So
we use basically

00:15:18.640 --> 00:15:22.360
Compute Engine because,
for me, as somebody

00:15:22.360 --> 00:15:24.850
who used to be
obsessive about firmware

00:15:24.850 --> 00:15:29.110
on individual components,
it's basically, OK, I

00:15:29.110 --> 00:15:31.660
have to let go of this
micromanaging a little bit.

00:15:31.660 --> 00:15:33.520
And the first step
is replicating

00:15:33.520 --> 00:15:35.170
the same architecture
on the cloud

00:15:35.170 --> 00:15:37.550
so I can basically
have a load balancer.

00:15:37.550 --> 00:15:39.640
I have my web servers, I
have my battle servers,

00:15:39.640 --> 00:15:43.270
I have Couchbase and
whatever services I use.

00:15:43.270 --> 00:15:47.570
Like Vertica is another database
that we use for analytics.

00:15:47.570 --> 00:15:49.152
And we use Reddit
for matchmaking

00:15:49.152 --> 00:15:50.110
and that kind of stuff.

00:15:50.110 --> 00:15:51.730
That all runs the same way.

00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:53.620
And the way we deploy
this is with Chef,

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:55.840
and that used to be the
same on the hardware

00:15:55.840 --> 00:15:58.300
as it is now, except for
it's much more flexible

00:15:58.300 --> 00:16:02.897
and goes faster and I can do it
with less impact on the cost.

00:16:02.897 --> 00:16:04.480
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
now I'm wondering,

00:16:04.480 --> 00:16:08.590
you were talking
about how passionate

00:16:08.590 --> 00:16:10.360
you were about hardware.

00:16:10.360 --> 00:16:13.665
Moving into the cloud, did
you use custom machines?

00:16:13.665 --> 00:16:15.040
Because you know,
it was like you

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:17.995
can tune exactly how much
RAM, how many spews and stuff

00:16:17.995 --> 00:16:19.210
like that you used?

00:16:19.210 --> 00:16:21.710
Or what kind of instances
did you move to?

00:16:21.710 --> 00:16:24.160
MICHAEL WILL: I basically
maximized memory

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:27.520
for the database
servers and computer

00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:28.960
basically to not
be bottlenecked.

00:16:28.960 --> 00:16:31.219
And basically, the approach
is get the maximum first

00:16:31.219 --> 00:16:33.760
and then tune it on later so
you notice what you really need.

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:35.749
MARK MANDEL: I think when
we talked previously,

00:16:35.749 --> 00:16:38.290
you got some nice notifications
from us saying that maybe you

00:16:38.290 --> 00:16:39.597
want to lower some usage there.

00:16:39.597 --> 00:16:40.430
MICHAEL WILL: Right.

00:16:40.430 --> 00:16:43.340
So I mean, the tools
that Google built

00:16:43.340 --> 00:16:47.120
are just great, especially
in comparison to other cloud

00:16:47.120 --> 00:16:47.620
vendors.

00:16:47.620 --> 00:16:50.650
I have to say, that's really
been one of the reasons

00:16:50.650 --> 00:16:53.110
why we went with Google,
is how quickly we

00:16:53.110 --> 00:16:55.150
were able to bring
up a prototype,

00:16:55.150 --> 00:16:57.606
and then say, hey, that
prototype, we will actually

00:16:57.606 --> 00:16:58.480
turn into production.

00:16:58.480 --> 00:17:00.440
Can we do this
tomorrow kind of thing.

00:17:00.440 --> 00:17:03.370
And so one of the tools
is the web interface,

00:17:03.370 --> 00:17:05.960
where you can basically
customize your machines

00:17:05.960 --> 00:17:09.569
and it even teaches you how
to use it in automated cases.

00:17:09.569 --> 00:17:11.319
There's a little button
that you can click

00:17:11.319 --> 00:17:12.730
and then you see
what the REST code

00:17:12.730 --> 00:17:14.829
would have been to achieve
what you are trying to do.

00:17:14.829 --> 00:17:16.069
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
It's super useful.

00:17:16.069 --> 00:17:17.950
MICHAEL WILL: So the
same web interface also

00:17:17.950 --> 00:17:20.910
shows you which machines that
you are running underutilized

00:17:20.910 --> 00:17:23.050
and it actually gives
you recommendations

00:17:23.050 --> 00:17:26.420
on paring it down to save
money, which is kind of cool.

00:17:26.420 --> 00:17:28.210
So it shows you, oh,
yeah, you have only

00:17:28.210 --> 00:17:30.230
been using 20% of
RAM of this machine.

00:17:30.230 --> 00:17:33.370
Why don't you use a custom
machine and you can save

00:17:33.370 --> 00:17:35.124
$50 a month on this machine?

00:17:35.124 --> 00:17:36.040
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:17:36.040 --> 00:17:39.820
You mentioned that, for
the database instances,

00:17:39.820 --> 00:17:42.490
you were maximizing RAM.

00:17:42.490 --> 00:17:43.840
What about storage?

00:17:43.840 --> 00:17:44.940
MICHAEL WILL: Storage.

00:17:44.940 --> 00:17:46.510
Well, you pay per
gigabyte, right?

00:17:46.510 --> 00:17:50.170
So originally, I also maximized
that until I realized the cost

00:17:50.170 --> 00:17:51.830
impact to its fullest.

00:17:51.830 --> 00:17:54.550
I mean, we did back of the
end calculations originally,

00:17:54.550 --> 00:17:58.060
but it really is a
different mix of costs

00:17:58.060 --> 00:18:00.220
that you have to be
aware of, especially

00:18:00.220 --> 00:18:01.750
if you plan to do
what we are doing,

00:18:01.750 --> 00:18:04.780
which is have some of
the instances turned off

00:18:04.780 --> 00:18:08.549
so you don't get billed
for the CPU time at all.

00:18:08.549 --> 00:18:10.090
But you can bring
them up really fast

00:18:10.090 --> 00:18:12.580
without having to reinstall
everything on them.

00:18:12.580 --> 00:18:14.900
And the storage that's
attached, however,

00:18:14.900 --> 00:18:17.050
is going to be built as if
it is running because it

00:18:17.050 --> 00:18:18.654
is occupying the space.

00:18:18.654 --> 00:18:19.570
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:18:19.570 --> 00:18:20.403
MICHAEL WILL: Right?

00:18:20.403 --> 00:18:22.530
So you pay for what
you're using, basically.

00:18:22.530 --> 00:18:25.045
FRANCESC CAMPOY: But were
you using, for the storage,

00:18:25.045 --> 00:18:27.194
were you using persistent disks?

00:18:27.194 --> 00:18:28.610
What kind of disks
were you using?

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:31.110
MICHAEL WILL: So Couchbase uses
network attached [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:31.110 --> 00:18:31.610
on disk.

00:18:31.610 --> 00:18:35.870
So SSDs for our lowest latency,
highest IOPS was the goal.

00:18:35.870 --> 00:18:37.600
And then the user--

00:18:37.600 --> 00:18:40.700
your Google servers are
called Google Storage in order

00:18:40.700 --> 00:18:42.080
to do backups.

00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:44.850
So instead of having a separate
file server like we used to do,

00:18:44.850 --> 00:18:47.150
we now basically just drop
it onto Google Storage,

00:18:47.150 --> 00:18:49.240
which is really easy to
use, also, with the tools

00:18:49.240 --> 00:18:50.185
that you have.

00:18:50.185 --> 00:18:52.060
It sets a web interface
that's a command line

00:18:52.060 --> 00:18:55.640
interface that's really easy
to integrate into your scripts.

00:18:55.640 --> 00:18:58.600
So I just run a few
lines of shell code

00:18:58.600 --> 00:19:02.830
and basically, I can spool data
from one system to the other

00:19:02.830 --> 00:19:03.560
with it.

00:19:03.560 --> 00:19:05.226
So what I'm doing is
the battle servers,

00:19:05.226 --> 00:19:08.470
they log out the actions that
happened and when people drop

00:19:08.470 --> 00:19:09.520
[INAUDIBLE] and so on.

00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:12.610
And all this data is basically
just a big large file.

00:19:12.610 --> 00:19:15.220
And I just have a
Cron script running

00:19:15.220 --> 00:19:17.830
that drops it into a
directory on Google Storage.

00:19:17.830 --> 00:19:19.390
And then I have a
different system

00:19:19.390 --> 00:19:22.000
that, again in the Cron
job, just takes everything

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:25.120
that it finds there
and then takes it apart

00:19:25.120 --> 00:19:28.956
and inserts it into Vertica as
comma-separated value files,

00:19:28.956 --> 00:19:30.580
which gives you the
highest throughput.

00:19:30.580 --> 00:19:31.610
MARK MANDEL: Actually,
you just you just

00:19:31.610 --> 00:19:33.310
brought up Vertica,
which is, I know,

00:19:33.310 --> 00:19:35.027
a big data processing tool.

00:19:35.027 --> 00:19:36.610
MICHAEL WILL: Yeah
It's like BigQuery,

00:19:36.610 --> 00:19:39.550
but it's for a smaller
capacity if you want.

00:19:39.550 --> 00:19:42.040
It's basically a
relational database

00:19:42.040 --> 00:19:43.140
that's column-oriented.

00:19:43.140 --> 00:19:45.100
That means inserts
are really slow,

00:19:45.100 --> 00:19:46.690
but selects are super fast.

00:19:46.690 --> 00:19:48.890
So if I run a query
on a billion rows,

00:19:48.890 --> 00:19:51.760
I can get a response in
under two seconds, sometimes

00:19:51.760 --> 00:19:52.780
under one second.

00:19:52.780 --> 00:19:54.914
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And that's
used more for analytics

00:19:54.914 --> 00:19:57.330
on what happened previously,
before, so it's not real time

00:19:57.330 --> 00:19:57.490
or anything.

00:19:57.490 --> 00:19:57.820
MICHAEL WILL: Right.

00:19:57.820 --> 00:19:58.870
It's an offline thing.

00:19:58.870 --> 00:20:01.600
So basically, the game
emits events like, oh,

00:20:01.600 --> 00:20:06.730
Player X purchased this or did
that action or tried to cheat

00:20:06.730 --> 00:20:08.520
or whatever we
detect in the game.

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:10.650
And it basically gets
collected and then

00:20:10.650 --> 00:20:12.870
we run analytics on it.

00:20:12.870 --> 00:20:17.134
We also use an external service
called Periscope for basically

00:20:17.134 --> 00:20:18.300
graphing that kind of stuff.

00:20:18.300 --> 00:20:21.990
So you can have your queries in
there and get nice dashboards.

00:20:21.990 --> 00:20:25.184
And it basically talks directly
to our Vertica back end.

00:20:25.184 --> 00:20:26.100
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:20:26.100 --> 00:20:27.270
I have so many
questions right now.

00:20:27.270 --> 00:20:28.853
MARK MANDEL: I have
lots of questions.

00:20:28.853 --> 00:20:29.470
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:29.470 --> 00:20:31.553
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So the
first question that I have

00:20:31.553 --> 00:20:34.490
is, OK, so you're using
SSDs to store the database,

00:20:34.490 --> 00:20:36.540
but you're also storing
some of that information

00:20:36.540 --> 00:20:37.740
into cloud storage.

00:20:37.740 --> 00:20:40.140
Then you read from
cloud storage,

00:20:40.140 --> 00:20:41.910
kind of like Map Reduce
style or whatever,

00:20:41.910 --> 00:20:43.650
like a big batch process.

00:20:43.650 --> 00:20:45.320
And then you store
that into Vertica.

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:45.700
MICHAEL WILL: Right.

00:20:45.700 --> 00:20:46.440
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Is that correct?

00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:48.315
MICHAEL WILL: That's
kind of how it is, yeah,

00:20:48.315 --> 00:20:51.801
in terms of getting analytics
data into a queryable system.

00:20:51.801 --> 00:20:53.550
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
once it's in Vertica,

00:20:53.550 --> 00:20:56.430
you're actually
starting again on SSDs,

00:20:56.430 --> 00:20:57.817
so the access is really fast.

00:20:57.817 --> 00:20:58.650
MICHAEL WILL: Right.

00:20:58.650 --> 00:20:58.860
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:20:58.860 --> 00:20:59.359
Cool.

00:20:59.359 --> 00:21:00.720
So my questions are--

00:21:00.720 --> 00:21:01.697
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:01.697 --> 00:21:03.280
MICHAEL WILL: And
then RAM, of course,

00:21:03.280 --> 00:21:05.405
because the Vertica
servers have lots of RAM.

00:21:05.405 --> 00:21:07.530
FRANCESC CAMPOY: In order
to make it actually fast.

00:21:07.530 --> 00:21:08.190
MICHAEL WILL: Yes.

00:21:08.190 --> 00:21:09.150
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
my first question

00:21:09.150 --> 00:21:11.310
is, when you're storing
on cloud storage,

00:21:11.310 --> 00:21:13.770
then it means that basically
you read it only once,

00:21:13.770 --> 00:21:15.990
and then you just
leave it there forever?

00:21:15.990 --> 00:21:18.960
Are you using something
like Nearline or Coldline,

00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:21.464
or have you considered
these things?

00:21:21.464 --> 00:21:23.880
MICHAEL WILL: We have considered
it, but time is precious,

00:21:23.880 --> 00:21:25.740
so what we do is
basically we just

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:28.560
have it on whatever the
default was at the time.

00:21:28.560 --> 00:21:30.530
And then we will
tune that later.

00:21:30.530 --> 00:21:34.220
The main cost, really, is in
the actual SSDs, the network

00:21:34.220 --> 00:21:37.661
attached disk drives, more
than the Nearline storage,

00:21:37.661 --> 00:21:38.160
if you want.

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:39.076
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:21:39.076 --> 00:21:41.670
And then the second
question is, since you're

00:21:41.670 --> 00:21:44.860
doing that offline
batching process,

00:21:44.860 --> 00:21:46.270
how are you doing those?

00:21:46.270 --> 00:21:48.950
Is that also machines
running on GCE?

00:21:48.950 --> 00:21:49.700
MICHAEL WILL: Yep.

00:21:49.700 --> 00:21:51.399
Everything there runs on GCE.

00:21:51.399 --> 00:21:53.940
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And whenever
you're doing something offline,

00:21:53.940 --> 00:21:55.380
my question is always the same.

00:21:55.380 --> 00:21:58.620
Are you using preemptible
machines or, again,

00:21:58.620 --> 00:22:00.900
it's something that you
might consider later on?

00:22:00.900 --> 00:22:01.858
MICHAEL WILL: Beats me.

00:22:01.858 --> 00:22:06.090
I mean, I want the systems to
be as reliable as possible.

00:22:06.090 --> 00:22:09.540
That's still the system
engineer talking, right?

00:22:09.540 --> 00:22:12.150
So I see them migrate
every now and then.

00:22:12.150 --> 00:22:15.010
And for a real-time game, that's
actually not really great.

00:22:15.010 --> 00:22:17.490
But it works fairly
well, so it's OK.

00:22:17.490 --> 00:22:19.380
And in particular,
what I've seen

00:22:19.380 --> 00:22:23.190
is that you hot migrate the
image and within a second,

00:22:23.190 --> 00:22:25.074
it can respond again,
or a few seconds.

00:22:25.074 --> 00:22:26.490
And I think that
only works really

00:22:26.490 --> 00:22:28.920
because the image doesn't
contain all the data,

00:22:28.920 --> 00:22:30.420
but the network
attached disks can

00:22:30.420 --> 00:22:33.090
be pointed to from the
new location easily.

00:22:33.090 --> 00:22:34.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Then what you're

00:22:34.980 --> 00:22:36.450
doing-- that offline
batch actually

00:22:36.450 --> 00:22:38.610
needs to be active all
of the time, right?

00:22:38.610 --> 00:22:41.099
So it's not like an offline
thing that it runs every day.

00:22:41.099 --> 00:22:42.015
It runs continuously--

00:22:42.015 --> 00:22:43.130
MICHAEL WILL: It runs
continuously, yeah.

00:22:43.130 --> 00:22:44.750
It's a constant feeding process.

00:22:44.750 --> 00:22:47.107
It's just that it's decoupled
from the actual gameplay.

00:22:47.107 --> 00:22:47.940
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:22:47.940 --> 00:22:49.170
That makes total sense.

00:22:49.170 --> 00:22:50.003
MICHAEL WILL: Right.

00:22:50.003 --> 00:22:52.280
MARK MANDEL: So actually
this is interesting, too.

00:22:52.280 --> 00:22:55.200
From first glance, talking about
what we were talking about,

00:22:55.200 --> 00:22:56.640
everything is sort of GCE.

00:22:56.640 --> 00:22:59.460
You're running your own
sort of analytics pipeline.

00:22:59.460 --> 00:23:01.740
Probably the only managed
service you're using

00:23:01.740 --> 00:23:04.350
is Cloud Storage and
maybe some load balancing.

00:23:04.350 --> 00:23:06.944
Was that a direct
choice to basically use

00:23:06.944 --> 00:23:08.610
more of our infrastructure
as a service?

00:23:08.610 --> 00:23:11.047
Or what was the
reasoning behind that?

00:23:11.047 --> 00:23:12.630
MICHAEL WILL: Well,
the first approach

00:23:12.630 --> 00:23:16.110
was, I have this hardware
architecture that

00:23:16.110 --> 00:23:17.596
runs on bare metal.

00:23:17.596 --> 00:23:19.470
And now, how do I make
this run on the cloud?

00:23:19.470 --> 00:23:21.928
And then what services are
there that could make it easier?

00:23:21.928 --> 00:23:23.580
So we were even
looking at replacing

00:23:23.580 --> 00:23:24.900
Couchbase with data store.

00:23:24.900 --> 00:23:27.960
But of course, our code
makes too many requests,

00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:30.270
so that's not really feasible
at this point in time.

00:23:30.270 --> 00:23:33.690
Because before, it was free and
now that would be transaction--

00:23:33.690 --> 00:23:35.940
each transaction
costing a little bit.

00:23:35.940 --> 00:23:37.330
And so we [INAUDIBLE].

00:23:37.330 --> 00:23:39.060
We also looked a
lot into BigQuery,

00:23:39.060 --> 00:23:41.750
because Vertica
machines eventually

00:23:41.750 --> 00:23:42.867
will run out of space.

00:23:42.867 --> 00:23:44.700
And that's the promise
of higher performance

00:23:44.700 --> 00:23:47.550
by having more hardware
and resources front ended

00:23:47.550 --> 00:23:49.140
from the Google side.

00:23:49.140 --> 00:23:52.120
And so that didn't pan
out in the first iteration

00:23:52.120 --> 00:23:53.610
so we didn't use that.

00:23:53.610 --> 00:23:57.630
But we needed to replace the
file server with [? eval ?]

00:23:57.630 --> 00:23:58.260
file server.

00:23:58.260 --> 00:24:00.330
But then we saw that
it's much easier

00:24:00.330 --> 00:24:03.180
to use with Google Storage and
it's also kind of unbounded.

00:24:03.180 --> 00:24:04.190
I can just flow edit.

00:24:04.190 --> 00:24:06.990
It never says, oh, disk
four, unlike the file server

00:24:06.990 --> 00:24:08.107
that needs babysitting.

00:24:08.107 --> 00:24:09.690
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
During the migration,

00:24:09.690 --> 00:24:12.780
did you just move the whole
thing all of a sudden?

00:24:12.780 --> 00:24:14.550
Like, you just got
the whole system

00:24:14.550 --> 00:24:18.170
stopped from your on-prem
and moved it to the Cloud?

00:24:18.170 --> 00:24:20.205
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:24:20.205 --> 00:24:22.080
MICHAEL WILL: We were
actually in development

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:23.850
when we made a decision
on where to run.

00:24:23.850 --> 00:24:27.000
So we had previous games that
were running on bare metal

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:29.520
and that still are, that
we have left behind when

00:24:29.520 --> 00:24:31.440
we moved companies around.

00:24:31.440 --> 00:24:33.340
And those are
running great there,

00:24:33.340 --> 00:24:35.770
but I don't have to worry
about those anymore.

00:24:35.770 --> 00:24:39.320
And so the new
approach was, OK, we

00:24:39.320 --> 00:24:41.100
could do it the
old fashioned way

00:24:41.100 --> 00:24:42.330
or we can do it the new way.

00:24:42.330 --> 00:24:44.760
And what really
brings us the value

00:24:44.760 --> 00:24:48.870
besides the nice tools
is that we can actually

00:24:48.870 --> 00:24:54.210
do a lot more load testing and
development, where you can just

00:24:54.210 --> 00:24:56.760
put together a copy
of the live system

00:24:56.760 --> 00:24:58.890
or even a bigger system
than the live system

00:24:58.890 --> 00:25:01.860
and run it completely
independent of the live system.

00:25:01.860 --> 00:25:04.630
And load test it
for a few hours--

00:25:04.630 --> 00:25:05.464
30, 40 or so hours--

00:25:05.464 --> 00:25:07.921
and then you shut them back
down and you don't pay for them

00:25:07.921 --> 00:25:08.800
anymore after that.

00:25:08.800 --> 00:25:10.630
You can just delete
them and they're gone.

00:25:10.630 --> 00:25:12.370
Whereas, in the
previous approach,

00:25:12.370 --> 00:25:15.700
we would have to have some
dedicated test hardware

00:25:15.700 --> 00:25:18.160
and would always have to
extrapolate what it probably

00:25:18.160 --> 00:25:20.710
would be like in the
bigger version and so on.

00:25:20.710 --> 00:25:23.260
So it kind of opens
up possibilities that

00:25:23.260 --> 00:25:24.970
just were not there before.

00:25:24.970 --> 00:25:27.460
FRANCESC CAMPOY: When
you're doing these tests,

00:25:27.460 --> 00:25:29.320
how do you measure
the performance?

00:25:29.320 --> 00:25:32.270
Are you using New Relic
for this and tracking--

00:25:32.270 --> 00:25:34.020
MICHAEL WILL: New Relic
is one part of it.

00:25:34.020 --> 00:25:36.950
But New Relic is looking
at individual transactions.

00:25:36.950 --> 00:25:40.690
But what I'm looking for
is volume and throughput.

00:25:40.690 --> 00:25:44.170
And so one open source tool
that's really useful for that

00:25:44.170 --> 00:25:45.114
is JMeter.

00:25:45.114 --> 00:25:46.030
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:25:46.030 --> 00:25:48.610
MICHAEL WILL: It's
written in Java

00:25:48.610 --> 00:25:52.890
and it has a nice
Java UI for basically

00:25:52.890 --> 00:25:54.430
making test plans and so on.

00:25:54.430 --> 00:25:57.810
And you run the system
with just one web server,

00:25:57.810 --> 00:25:59.830
print out all the
queries that come--

00:25:59.830 --> 00:26:04.030
all the posts that go through it
when you test with your phone.

00:26:04.030 --> 00:26:06.310
And you basically see, OK,
when my phone installs,

00:26:06.310 --> 00:26:08.050
these are the
steps it will take.

00:26:08.050 --> 00:26:11.130
And then you can convert
those into, basically,

00:26:11.130 --> 00:26:15.970
a JMeter test blend that can
do 1,000 of them in parallel.

00:26:15.970 --> 00:26:18.260
And then you run some of
them on a few servers,

00:26:18.260 --> 00:26:21.460
and then you get basically
4,000 requests coming in,

00:26:21.460 --> 00:26:23.540
and you run it over
and over again.

00:26:23.540 --> 00:26:25.960
MARK MANDEL: So is that just
HTTP requests or is that also

00:26:25.960 --> 00:26:28.249
stress-testing your battle
servers, which are in C++?

00:26:28.249 --> 00:26:29.540
MICHAEL WILL: It's all implied.

00:26:29.540 --> 00:26:32.440
So the activity that you do is
just like the phones would do.

00:26:32.440 --> 00:26:34.479
So it has consequences.

00:26:34.479 --> 00:26:35.770
It's the same thing, basically.

00:26:35.770 --> 00:26:37.180
It starts the battles.

00:26:37.180 --> 00:26:38.800
It doesn't do the
randomized stuff

00:26:38.800 --> 00:26:40.630
that real players would
do and there won't

00:26:40.630 --> 00:26:41.890
be the same kind of delays.

00:26:41.890 --> 00:26:46.390
But in order to get a worst
case scenario done, if I really

00:26:46.390 --> 00:26:49.900
have $1 million [INAUDIBLE] a
day of people that really play

00:26:49.900 --> 00:26:53.367
a lot, basically, then I can
see, how many servers do I

00:26:53.367 --> 00:26:53.950
need for that?

00:26:53.950 --> 00:26:55.780
And will it go down
or will it survive?

00:26:55.780 --> 00:26:58.180
And so I'm looking at overall
throughput of installs.

00:26:58.180 --> 00:27:00.735
And then I identify weak
points and I see, oh, yeah, I

00:27:00.735 --> 00:27:02.920
needs more of my battle
servers than I thought.

00:27:02.920 --> 00:27:05.830
Or I need to look at some of the
requests slowing down suddenly

00:27:05.830 --> 00:27:08.530
because they are focused too
much on [? Reddits ?] and it's

00:27:08.530 --> 00:27:10.095
overloaded, stuff like that.

00:27:10.095 --> 00:27:11.011
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:27:11.011 --> 00:27:13.480
So I wonder, other than
for the flexibility

00:27:13.480 --> 00:27:15.260
of being able to
scale up and down

00:27:15.260 --> 00:27:18.025
and add systems for
testing and QA and stuff

00:27:18.025 --> 00:27:21.400
like that, what are other
benefits that you've seen,

00:27:21.400 --> 00:27:23.830
or even what are the
problems that you've seen,

00:27:23.830 --> 00:27:25.875
by migrating to the cloud?

00:27:25.875 --> 00:27:28.000
MICHAEL WILL: Challenges
of migrating to the cloud,

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:30.920
of course, at first
is psychological.

00:27:30.920 --> 00:27:32.233
Like there's this, oh--

00:27:32.233 --> 00:27:32.680
MARK MANDEL: Your dear machines.

00:27:32.680 --> 00:27:35.050
MICHAEL WILL: --is this
going to go away overnight?

00:27:35.050 --> 00:27:37.360
And then what do I do?

00:27:37.360 --> 00:27:41.840
So of course, we have backups
that are basically proven.

00:27:41.840 --> 00:27:43.707
And I prove it to myself
every now and then

00:27:43.707 --> 00:27:45.790
that I can build a new
system with the backup data

00:27:45.790 --> 00:27:48.900
and it actually still works,
so that's a good thing.

00:27:48.900 --> 00:27:52.090
That again, is something that
the cloud really supports.

00:27:52.090 --> 00:27:53.740
The other challenge
that we found

00:27:53.740 --> 00:27:57.950
was I was kind of
optimizing on hardware

00:27:57.950 --> 00:27:59.950
and I was thinking I'd
do the same optimizations

00:27:59.950 --> 00:28:02.750
on the cloud, but sometimes
simpler is just better.

00:28:02.750 --> 00:28:04.570
So for the couch base
server, for example,

00:28:04.570 --> 00:28:07.900
I was used to having two rate
controllers, four volumes that

00:28:07.900 --> 00:28:10.150
are giving me maximum
IOPS, basically,

00:28:10.150 --> 00:28:11.770
if I stripe across them.

00:28:11.770 --> 00:28:14.140
And so I tried a similar
approach on the cloud.

00:28:14.140 --> 00:28:17.500
And, in theory, that should
really work, but in reality,

00:28:17.500 --> 00:28:20.039
it was actually better to
just use a single volume

00:28:20.039 --> 00:28:21.580
that is then larger
and that is being

00:28:21.580 --> 00:28:25.030
handled by Google on the back
end in order to maximize IOPS.

00:28:25.030 --> 00:28:28.000
And so one of the lessons
learned is basically,

00:28:28.000 --> 00:28:30.382
don't try so hard to do
the same optimizations

00:28:30.382 --> 00:28:32.590
because they might actually
be in the way of success.

00:28:32.590 --> 00:28:33.506
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:28:33.506 --> 00:28:35.920
Go for the simple thing and
then, as you were saying,

00:28:35.920 --> 00:28:39.420
test it and find how to
make it faster later.

00:28:39.420 --> 00:28:40.270
That's cool.

00:28:40.270 --> 00:28:40.915
MICHAEL WILL:
Another one is don't

00:28:40.915 --> 00:28:42.790
try to use all the
services up front.

00:28:42.790 --> 00:28:44.770
Like I said, we
were learning a lot

00:28:44.770 --> 00:28:47.590
and spending a lot of time
on which services to use

00:28:47.590 --> 00:28:50.650
and the approach to first
shift it into the cloud

00:28:50.650 --> 00:28:53.680
and then disassemble it
later and look at, well,

00:28:53.680 --> 00:28:55.270
if I take this
apart, can I actually

00:28:55.270 --> 00:28:56.822
use some of the services?

00:28:56.822 --> 00:28:59.030
Initially, I was really
thinking about a file server.

00:28:59.030 --> 00:29:00.610
That one I could
rule out quickly.

00:29:00.610 --> 00:29:02.939
But the Couchbase, that
will take us a while

00:29:02.939 --> 00:29:04.480
to re-architect so
that we don't have

00:29:04.480 --> 00:29:06.616
so many transactional
costs, for example.

00:29:06.616 --> 00:29:07.990
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
I wonder, you've

00:29:07.990 --> 00:29:11.999
migrated up to a pretty
cloud-oriented point.

00:29:11.999 --> 00:29:13.540
You have all of
these servers running

00:29:13.540 --> 00:29:14.920
on the cloud and everything.

00:29:14.920 --> 00:29:19.040
Are you planning on feature
migrations to other services?

00:29:19.040 --> 00:29:20.830
What is the future roadmap?

00:29:20.830 --> 00:29:23.350
MICHAEL WILL: Well, the future
is always wide open, right?

00:29:23.350 --> 00:29:25.480
So we are fast-moving,
in terms of the features

00:29:25.480 --> 00:29:26.260
that we're using.

00:29:26.260 --> 00:29:29.332
I was looking just recently
at the translation API.

00:29:29.332 --> 00:29:30.790
So I was thinking
like, oh, we have

00:29:30.790 --> 00:29:32.440
all these international
chats going.

00:29:32.440 --> 00:29:34.630
There are chat rooms in
Italian, some in Chinese,

00:29:34.630 --> 00:29:35.800
some in Arabic.

00:29:35.800 --> 00:29:38.680
Wouldn't it be nice if you
could look at, basically,

00:29:38.680 --> 00:29:40.600
what they are saying,
besides having

00:29:40.600 --> 00:29:43.000
to have language domain
experts in the company that

00:29:43.000 --> 00:29:44.140
can tell me?

00:29:44.140 --> 00:29:45.820
And so I was looking at that.

00:29:45.820 --> 00:29:47.470
So that's one interesting thing.

00:29:47.470 --> 00:29:48.890
And then, of
course, we're always

00:29:48.890 --> 00:29:50.890
curious in learning more
about machine learning.

00:29:50.890 --> 00:29:53.770
And every day, you come up with
new services and new stuff.

00:29:53.770 --> 00:29:55.600
So that's what we
like about Google,

00:29:55.600 --> 00:29:58.870
in general, is you guys are
fast-moving, just like we are,

00:29:58.870 --> 00:30:00.430
and very innovative
and cutting edge.

00:30:00.430 --> 00:30:03.559
And so new options
always come up.

00:30:03.559 --> 00:30:06.100
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I guess we're
running out of time, according

00:30:06.100 --> 00:30:08.270
to Mark's hand gestures.

00:30:08.270 --> 00:30:10.260
So is there anything
else that you'd

00:30:10.260 --> 00:30:12.730
like to mention that
we might have missed?

00:30:12.730 --> 00:30:14.740
MICHAEL WILL: Well,
an anecdote would be--

00:30:14.740 --> 00:30:16.200
and I hinted to that earlier--

00:30:16.200 --> 00:30:19.660
the tools that we use to
basically prove to ourselves

00:30:19.660 --> 00:30:21.340
that this is easy
to use and it was

00:30:21.340 --> 00:30:24.610
much easier than other cloud
providers led to the situation

00:30:24.610 --> 00:30:27.960
that we basically had a system
that was meant as a prototype,

00:30:27.960 --> 00:30:30.310
but then was going to
be put into production.

00:30:30.310 --> 00:30:33.490
And we were like, hey,
can you do this tomorrow?

00:30:33.490 --> 00:30:36.490
And then, they're like, well,
you have infinite resources,

00:30:36.490 --> 00:30:38.650
but you have to
negotiate a quota first.

00:30:38.650 --> 00:30:41.860
And quota's basically your
framework for, how much will

00:30:41.860 --> 00:30:42.940
you potentially use?

00:30:42.940 --> 00:30:44.976
And it's kind of
reserved resources

00:30:44.976 --> 00:30:46.600
so that they don't
run out of resources

00:30:46.600 --> 00:30:47.950
just when you need them.

00:30:47.950 --> 00:30:53.200
And so I think engaging early
to increase the quota when

00:30:53.200 --> 00:30:56.050
you want to go live,
that's an important thing.

00:30:56.050 --> 00:30:59.590
And yeah, that was maybe--

00:30:59.590 --> 00:31:03.550
one take-away is it's so
easy that you could just move

00:31:03.550 --> 00:31:05.275
right into production quickly.

00:31:05.275 --> 00:31:07.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Are you working
with customer engineers, sales

00:31:07.900 --> 00:31:10.790
engineers, or any
kind of those people

00:31:10.790 --> 00:31:14.140
in order to do those quotas,
or is it completely automated?

00:31:14.140 --> 00:31:16.300
MICHAEL WILL: Well, it's
somewhat bureaucratic.

00:31:16.300 --> 00:31:20.120
There's a form and you fill it
out and then you talk about it.

00:31:20.120 --> 00:31:22.750
But you have very
strong technical people,

00:31:22.750 --> 00:31:25.400
and also a really
good sales guy that I

00:31:25.400 --> 00:31:28.840
work with who understands
the technology just as

00:31:28.840 --> 00:31:29.560
good as I do.

00:31:29.560 --> 00:31:31.990
And so it was really easy
to talk with him about, OK,

00:31:31.990 --> 00:31:33.782
this is important to
us and that one isn't.

00:31:33.782 --> 00:31:35.531
And then he made it
happen on the back end

00:31:35.531 --> 00:31:36.448
and pushed it forward.

00:31:36.448 --> 00:31:38.364
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I guess
that ties pretty well

00:31:38.364 --> 00:31:40.930
to all of our other episodes
on customer engineers and sales

00:31:40.930 --> 00:31:44.050
engineers and customer
reliability engineers and all

00:31:44.050 --> 00:31:44.760
that stuff.

00:31:44.760 --> 00:31:45.505
MICHAEL WILL: Lots of resources.

00:31:45.505 --> 00:31:46.120
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:31:46.120 --> 00:31:48.050
MICHAEL WILL: That's
generally what I like.

00:31:48.050 --> 00:31:49.008
MARK MANDEL: Wonderful.

00:31:49.008 --> 00:31:51.040
And also, make sure we
get all the plugs in.

00:31:51.040 --> 00:31:52.470
You've got-- the app is out.

00:31:52.470 --> 00:31:55.720
So "Guardian Kingdoms" is out on
iOS and Android is coming soon?

00:31:55.720 --> 00:31:56.500
MICHAEL WILL: Yes.

00:31:56.500 --> 00:31:57.916
Definitely by the
end of the year,

00:31:57.916 --> 00:31:59.300
you will see an Android version.

00:31:59.300 --> 00:31:59.530
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:31:59.530 --> 00:31:59.890
Wonderful.

00:31:59.890 --> 00:32:01.973
We'll have links in the
show notes to the website,

00:32:01.973 --> 00:32:04.830
so everyone who wants to go
play the game can find it.

00:32:04.830 --> 00:32:05.830
MICHAEL WILL: All right.

00:32:05.830 --> 00:32:06.935
Play "Guardian Kingdoms."

00:32:06.935 --> 00:32:07.810
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yep.

00:32:07.810 --> 00:32:08.346
[LAUGHTER]

00:32:08.346 --> 00:32:09.040
MARK MANDEL: Fantastic.

00:32:09.040 --> 00:32:11.170
Well, Will, thank you so much
for spending time with us

00:32:11.170 --> 00:32:12.961
and answering all our
ridiculous questions,

00:32:12.961 --> 00:32:14.412
or maybe not so ridiculous.

00:32:14.412 --> 00:32:16.120
We really appreciate
you taking the time.

00:32:16.120 --> 00:32:17.230
MICHAEL WILL: Yeah, same here.

00:32:17.230 --> 00:32:19.070
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Thanks
so much to Michael Will

00:32:19.070 --> 00:32:20.620
to spend some time
today and tell us

00:32:20.620 --> 00:32:23.410
a little bit about the whole
experience of migrating

00:32:23.410 --> 00:32:27.060
something as hard to migrate--

00:32:27.060 --> 00:32:31.540
a game environment-- from
very well-known hardware

00:32:31.540 --> 00:32:34.120
to the cloud, and how everything
worked out really well.

00:32:34.120 --> 00:32:34.845
Very, very cool.

00:32:34.845 --> 00:32:35.720
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:32:35.720 --> 00:32:36.970
Yeah, thanks again to Michael.

00:32:36.970 --> 00:32:38.650
It was a really good
story and I think

00:32:38.650 --> 00:32:41.260
he had a really good
take on the right steps

00:32:41.260 --> 00:32:44.650
to take when migrating and when
to make those trade-offs about,

00:32:44.650 --> 00:32:45.790
do I use the new things?

00:32:45.790 --> 00:32:47.860
Do I just continue
with what I know,

00:32:47.860 --> 00:32:50.662
and then just make small
steps, which they did.

00:32:50.662 --> 00:32:52.870
I thought that was a really,
really interesting take.

00:32:52.870 --> 00:32:54.750
Why don't we move on to
our question of the week?

00:32:54.750 --> 00:32:56.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
And so the question

00:32:56.250 --> 00:33:00.730
is a pretty general one,
but that has many answers,

00:33:00.730 --> 00:33:04.270
and has a new one since
not that long ago.

00:33:04.270 --> 00:33:07.450
So the question is, OK, how
do I secure an App Engine

00:33:07.450 --> 00:33:08.070
application?

00:33:08.070 --> 00:33:11.080
And by securing it, I
mean that only the people

00:33:11.080 --> 00:33:15.790
I want to access the web page
will be able to access it.

00:33:15.790 --> 00:33:18.172
That is the only one that
we care about right now.

00:33:18.172 --> 00:33:18.880
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:33:18.880 --> 00:33:19.540
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
There is one, which

00:33:19.540 --> 00:33:22.570
is the typical one that I've
been using forever, which

00:33:22.570 --> 00:33:24.910
is basically on your
[INAUDIBLE],, you can say,

00:33:24.910 --> 00:33:29.770
login required, or even login
admin, for a specific endpoint.

00:33:29.770 --> 00:33:32.170
So you can say, well,
this web page here, you

00:33:32.170 --> 00:33:34.490
need to be logged
in to access it.

00:33:34.490 --> 00:33:37.660
Now, if someone tries to access
it and they're not logged in,

00:33:37.660 --> 00:33:41.932
what happens is that the App
Engine framework will actually

00:33:41.932 --> 00:33:45.010
re-direct that to
a login page that

00:33:45.010 --> 00:33:47.880
only works for Google
accounts, and then you

00:33:47.880 --> 00:33:48.880
need to log in in there.

00:33:48.880 --> 00:33:50.920
And by Google accounts,
I also mean Google Apps.

00:33:50.920 --> 00:33:53.332
So if you have at
GCPpodcast.com,

00:33:53.332 --> 00:33:54.540
for instance, is one of them.

00:33:54.540 --> 00:33:57.860
You will log in and then go
back to the same web page.

00:33:57.860 --> 00:34:00.580
And this time, you will be
authenticated so it will work.

00:34:00.580 --> 00:34:03.430
Similarly, you can also
say that login is not

00:34:03.430 --> 00:34:05.290
required, but admin.

00:34:05.290 --> 00:34:08.800
And when you do this, not only
you need to be authenticated,

00:34:08.800 --> 00:34:12.460
but you need to be also an
owner of the project where

00:34:12.460 --> 00:34:15.110
that App Engine is hosted.

00:34:15.110 --> 00:34:17.360
So for instance,
let's say that we

00:34:17.360 --> 00:34:20.199
added some kind of
authentication for GCP Podcast.

00:34:20.199 --> 00:34:23.785
Everybody could authenticate
to those, but only Mark

00:34:23.785 --> 00:34:28.179
and I would be able to use those
that are marked as login admin.

00:34:28.179 --> 00:34:29.949
These are also very
useful for when you're

00:34:29.949 --> 00:34:31.309
doing things like Cron jobs.

00:34:31.309 --> 00:34:32.350
MARK MANDEL: There we go.

00:34:32.350 --> 00:34:33.760
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cron jobs--
this is one of the things

00:34:33.760 --> 00:34:35.991
that you really don't want
anyone to be able to hit.

00:34:35.991 --> 00:34:36.699
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:34:36.699 --> 00:34:39.032
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Because, in
general, they're expensive.

00:34:39.032 --> 00:34:40.540
You don't want
someone to go and hit

00:34:40.540 --> 00:34:43.060
like that once a second
for the rest of the day.

00:34:43.060 --> 00:34:44.852
MARK MANDEL: And
obscurity is not security.

00:34:44.852 --> 00:34:45.976
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It is not.

00:34:45.976 --> 00:34:47.710
It can help a little
bit, but someone

00:34:47.710 --> 00:34:49.550
will find it at some point.

00:34:49.550 --> 00:34:51.969
So yeah, it is important
to be able to do required,

00:34:51.969 --> 00:34:53.400
admin, stuff like that.

00:34:53.400 --> 00:34:54.650
So that is one of the options.

00:34:54.650 --> 00:34:55.870
MARK MANDEL: And it's
worth noting that that only

00:34:55.870 --> 00:34:57.080
works with Google accounts.

00:34:57.080 --> 00:34:57.490
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yes.

00:34:57.490 --> 00:34:59.170
It only works with
Google accounts.

00:34:59.170 --> 00:35:00.262
A Google Apps account.

00:35:00.262 --> 00:35:00.970
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:35:00.970 --> 00:35:01.886
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:35:01.886 --> 00:35:03.237
MARK MANDEL: So second way--

00:35:03.237 --> 00:35:04.570
I love what you've written here.

00:35:04.570 --> 00:35:06.170
You do you.

00:35:06.170 --> 00:35:06.945
I love that.

00:35:06.945 --> 00:35:08.320
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Everything goes.

00:35:08.320 --> 00:35:08.810
You do you.

00:35:08.810 --> 00:35:09.518
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:35:09.518 --> 00:35:12.070
So basically, build your own
authentication into your app.

00:35:12.070 --> 00:35:12.160
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:35:12.160 --> 00:35:13.450
MARK MANDEL: Maybe use
something like data store

00:35:13.450 --> 00:35:16.060
for the back end storage, build
your own OAuth provider, hook

00:35:16.060 --> 00:35:18.017
into existing OAuth providers.

00:35:18.017 --> 00:35:19.600
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I
stole your passport

00:35:19.600 --> 00:35:23.150
without even encrypting them,
[INAUDIBLE] your [? salt. ?]

00:35:23.150 --> 00:35:25.480
[LAUGHTER]

00:35:25.480 --> 00:35:27.920
MARK MANDEL: Whatever level
that you are comfortable with.

00:35:27.920 --> 00:35:28.540
Please don't do that.

00:35:28.540 --> 00:35:29.080
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Do not do that.

00:35:29.080 --> 00:35:30.360
MARK MANDEL: Do not do that.

00:35:30.360 --> 00:35:32.260
So more work,
obviously, but that

00:35:32.260 --> 00:35:34.270
enables you full
control over everything

00:35:34.270 --> 00:35:36.027
from how you want
people to authenticate

00:35:36.027 --> 00:35:37.360
and what you want to build into.

00:35:37.360 --> 00:35:39.443
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And also
following this same idea

00:35:39.443 --> 00:35:42.160
of using OAuth 2, you
do not need to implement

00:35:42.160 --> 00:35:43.670
the whole thing by yourself.

00:35:43.670 --> 00:35:45.950
O Auth 2 is kind of
hard to implement.

00:35:45.950 --> 00:35:47.830
It's never fun.

00:35:47.830 --> 00:35:50.560
But there's providers that
allow you to do these basically

00:35:50.560 --> 00:35:52.370
with zero effort.

00:35:52.370 --> 00:35:55.534
One of them-- my favorite
one-- is Firebase auth.

00:35:55.534 --> 00:35:56.950
Firebase authentication
will allow

00:35:56.950 --> 00:36:00.250
you to provide a very simple
way of doing authentication

00:36:00.250 --> 00:36:01.314
using OAuth 2.

00:36:01.314 --> 00:36:02.730
And not only with
Google accounts,

00:36:02.730 --> 00:36:04.330
but also Facebook, Twitter--

00:36:04.330 --> 00:36:06.212
anything They even
support GitHub and stuff.

00:36:06.212 --> 00:36:07.420
They support a bunch of them.

00:36:07.420 --> 00:36:08.140
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:36:08.140 --> 00:36:08.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
And you don't need

00:36:08.980 --> 00:36:10.720
to do anything on your site.

00:36:10.720 --> 00:36:12.970
Everything you do is
you receive a token

00:36:12.970 --> 00:36:16.870
and then you use a
library to try to identify

00:36:16.870 --> 00:36:19.760
what user is behind that token.

00:36:19.760 --> 00:36:20.920
Super cool.

00:36:20.920 --> 00:36:24.590
In a very similar way, Auth Zero
does something quite similar.

00:36:24.590 --> 00:36:26.830
So check those out if
what you want to do

00:36:26.830 --> 00:36:29.320
is authentication with many
different kinds of accounts

00:36:29.320 --> 00:36:32.296
and you don't really want to
tie yourself to one provider.

00:36:32.296 --> 00:36:34.420
MARK MANDEL: So we also
have this neat thing called

00:36:34.420 --> 00:36:36.035
the Identity Aware Proxy.

00:36:36.035 --> 00:36:36.910
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yes.

00:36:36.910 --> 00:36:39.400
Also known as
Beyond [? Corps. ?]

00:36:39.400 --> 00:36:40.960
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
mostly internally.

00:36:40.960 --> 00:36:42.100
But yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:36:42.100 --> 00:36:44.230
So Identity Aware Proxy
is actually kind of cool,

00:36:44.230 --> 00:36:46.540
in that it is a layer that
sits in front of App Engine,

00:36:46.540 --> 00:36:48.990
as well as some other products,
if you really want to,

00:36:48.990 --> 00:36:53.170
that enables you to basically
control complete access based

00:36:53.170 --> 00:36:54.926
on who these people are.

00:36:54.926 --> 00:36:56.800
So you can set up roles
and responsibilities.

00:36:56.800 --> 00:37:00.919
Again, this is linked to Google
accounts, so keep that in mind.

00:37:00.919 --> 00:37:03.460
That if somebody comes in who's
not meant to access this app,

00:37:03.460 --> 00:37:06.280
then they don't even
get to hit that URL.

00:37:06.280 --> 00:37:07.240
That never happens.

00:37:07.240 --> 00:37:08.850
And you can set up
a bunch of rules.

00:37:08.850 --> 00:37:11.350
This is nice because not only
can you apply it in one place,

00:37:11.350 --> 00:37:13.060
you could then apply
it in multiple places

00:37:13.060 --> 00:37:14.650
around your organization
from multiple apps,

00:37:14.650 --> 00:37:15.790
as well, which is quite nice.

00:37:15.790 --> 00:37:16.090
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:37:16.090 --> 00:37:16.949
It is really cool.

00:37:16.949 --> 00:37:18.490
Let's imagine that
you have a company

00:37:18.490 --> 00:37:21.520
and that company has four or
five different applications

00:37:21.520 --> 00:37:25.030
and you want to use the same
authentication schema for all

00:37:25.030 --> 00:37:27.540
of them, which makes sense.

00:37:27.540 --> 00:37:29.290
This is probably the
easiest way to do it.

00:37:29.290 --> 00:37:30.250
Rather than having
authentication

00:37:30.250 --> 00:37:32.200
in every single one of
those apps, what you do

00:37:32.200 --> 00:37:35.740
is you protect those apps with
the Cloud Identity Aware proxy,

00:37:35.740 --> 00:37:37.060
or actually you could do both.

00:37:37.060 --> 00:37:37.810
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:37:37.810 --> 00:37:38.390
You could do both.

00:37:38.390 --> 00:37:40.630
The nice thing, as well,
is the Identity Aware Proxy

00:37:40.630 --> 00:37:43.270
will pass through
who the user is.

00:37:43.270 --> 00:37:46.704
So it not only provides access
protection, but also has,

00:37:46.704 --> 00:37:47.620
basically, user login.

00:37:47.620 --> 00:37:49.510
You get that information
as it comes through.

00:37:49.510 --> 00:37:50.570
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
one of the cool things

00:37:50.570 --> 00:37:52.570
is that this is
something that will

00:37:52.570 --> 00:37:56.140
stop a request from
getting to your application

00:37:56.140 --> 00:37:58.730
in the first place, unless
it's been authenticated.

00:37:58.730 --> 00:37:59.620
So it's pretty good.

00:37:59.620 --> 00:38:03.190
App Engine, for instance,
if you keep on hitting it,

00:38:03.190 --> 00:38:05.110
you will have an
instance running.

00:38:05.110 --> 00:38:08.250
It might even scale up if
you got a lot of traffic.

00:38:08.250 --> 00:38:11.234
If that traffic is
not authenticated,

00:38:11.234 --> 00:38:13.650
that traffic will never hit
your instance, so you're good.

00:38:13.650 --> 00:38:15.108
MARK MANDEL: So
that's pretty cool.

00:38:15.108 --> 00:38:18.156
Now, if I want to lock it
down to just a section of IPs?

00:38:18.156 --> 00:38:19.030
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yes.

00:38:19.030 --> 00:38:21.220
So that's what we
call a firewall,

00:38:21.220 --> 00:38:23.230
and that's the new thing.

00:38:23.230 --> 00:38:27.010
We have an App Engine
firewall and it is as simple

00:38:27.010 --> 00:38:27.640
as it sounds.

00:38:27.640 --> 00:38:29.990
It is a firewall
where you can say,

00:38:29.990 --> 00:38:33.940
only requests coming from
this specific IP or this range

00:38:33.940 --> 00:38:36.300
of IPs is able to hit my app.

00:38:36.300 --> 00:38:38.080
If they're not coming
in from those IPs,

00:38:38.080 --> 00:38:39.170
they will be dropped.

00:38:39.170 --> 00:38:41.546
So your application will not
receive any of that traffic.

00:38:41.546 --> 00:38:42.503
MARK MANDEL: Very nice.

00:38:42.503 --> 00:38:44.290
I think if I wanted
to use these in combo,

00:38:44.290 --> 00:38:47.230
I would probably do something
like Cloud Identity Aware Proxy

00:38:47.230 --> 00:38:48.502
plus an App Engine firewall.

00:38:48.502 --> 00:38:50.210
So if I've got, say,
a corporate network,

00:38:50.210 --> 00:38:52.150
I know what my IP range
is, so I know, OK,

00:38:52.150 --> 00:38:55.390
this can only come from
within these IP ranges.

00:38:55.390 --> 00:38:57.910
And then IAP on top of that
so that I have both layers.

00:38:57.910 --> 00:39:00.370
Maybe if I accidentally
misconfigure IAP,

00:39:00.370 --> 00:39:01.900
I still have the
added protection

00:39:01.900 --> 00:39:03.820
of the IP range and vice versa.

00:39:03.820 --> 00:39:03.960
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:39:03.960 --> 00:39:04.501
I don't know.

00:39:04.501 --> 00:39:07.099
I think that Cloud Identity
Aware Proxy is secure enough.

00:39:07.099 --> 00:39:08.390
MARK MANDEL: It's pretty great.

00:39:08.390 --> 00:39:09.306
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:39:09.306 --> 00:39:11.025
But Cloud Identity
Aware Proxy, it

00:39:11.025 --> 00:39:12.860
makes it so you need
to authenticate.

00:39:12.860 --> 00:39:15.430
So probably what I would do
is use Cloud Identity Aware

00:39:15.430 --> 00:39:18.400
Proxy for all the things
where users are using it.

00:39:18.400 --> 00:39:20.830
But then use App Engine
firewall for when

00:39:20.830 --> 00:39:23.960
you have a different
system that is hitting you.

00:39:23.960 --> 00:39:25.960
And you know what IP
you're using, so it's like,

00:39:25.960 --> 00:39:27.070
only that can--

00:39:27.070 --> 00:39:29.520
kind of like a remote Cron
job or something that.

00:39:29.520 --> 00:39:30.040
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, you
could do that, too.

00:39:30.040 --> 00:39:32.440
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It
could help, I think.

00:39:32.440 --> 00:39:33.040
But yeah.

00:39:33.040 --> 00:39:33.580
MARK MANDEL: There's
probably lots

00:39:33.580 --> 00:39:34.930
